PRESCRIBED GREP (A1 gate on /sw.js), run first, real output:

  $ cd C:/ClaudeCode/routeflow/.claude/worktrees/rf-watchdog
  $ grep -rn "serviceWorker\|sw\.js" apps/mobile --include=*.ts --include=*.tsx --include=*.js -l --exclude-dir=node_modules
  (no output)
  EXIT=1

EMPTY -> per the ruling /sw.js joins the allow-list. (Cross-check: the only /sw.js reference in the repo's web tree is apps/web/components/ServiceWorkerRegistry.tsx:7 `navigator.serviceWorker.register("/sw.js", { scope: "/" })`, and apps/web/public/sw.js exists.)

Asset inventory used to build the list (`ls apps/web/public`): brand/ (routeflow-mark-64|180|192|512.png), marketing/ (retailer-technology.webp, stock-cutout-v3.webp, warehouse.webp), operator-manifest.json, buyer-manifest.json, sw.js. No public/video (ls exit 2) and `grep -rn '/video/' app components lib` = no hits. Only favicon is app/favicon.ico; no favicon.svg/png.

=== HUNK 1 — apps/web/lib/marketing-routes.ts (appended; header line reworded) ===
- Header line 2 now reads "The public marketing page paths - plus the auth entry points and the same-origin assets those pages need - in one place."
+ export const MARKETING_AUTH_PATHS = ["/signup", "/buyer/login", "/buyer/register"] as const;
    docblock states /login is deliberately ABSENT (mobile-web login is the phone operator entry point) and names app/(marketing)/components/auth-links.ts as the link source.
+ export const MARKETING_ASSET_PREFIXES = ["/brand/", "/marketing/"] as const;
+ export const MARKETING_ASSET_FILES = ["/operator-manifest.json", "/buyer-manifest.json", "/sw.js", "/favicon.ico"] as const;
    docblock records the grep verbatim as the /sw.js justification, states this is an explicit allow-list and never a *.json/*.js extension rule (the Expo build's own manifest/bundles must keep proxying), and says why /robots.txt + /sitemap.xml are NOT duplicated here (already in MARKETING_PATHS; a second copy is the L-072 drift this module exists to prevent).
+ export function isMarketingAsset(pathname: string): boolean {
+   return MARKETING_ASSET_PREFIXES.some((prefix) => pathname.startsWith(prefix)) ||
+     (MARKETING_ASSET_FILES as readonly string[]).includes(pathname);
+ }

=== HUNK 2 — apps/web/middleware.ts, imports ===
- import { MARKETING_PAGE_PATHS } from "@/lib/marketing-routes";
+ import { MARKETING_AUTH_PATHS, MARKETING_PAGE_PATHS, isMarketingAsset } from "@/lib/marketing-routes";

=== HUNK 3 — middleware.ts, after isMarketingPath (new module constants) ===
+ const MARKETING_AUTH_PATH_SET = new Set<string>(MARKETING_AUTH_PATHS);   // exact-match, /login not a member on purpose
+ export const MOBILE_APP_COOKIE = "rf-mobile-app";                         // exported for the test
+ const MOBILE_APP_COOKIE_MAX_AGE = 60 * 60 * 24 * 365;                     // 1 year
  with a docblock explaining that the Expo build keeps its session in AsyncStorage/SecureStore and writes no cookie, so the presence cookies are structurally invisible to the mobile-app population; httpOnly because it is a routing signal for the middleware only.

=== HUNK 4 — middleware.ts, the skip decision (A1 + A2 + A3) ===
  const signedIn = opAuthed || buyerAuthed;
+ const isProd = process.env.NODE_ENV === "production";        // hoisted (see note 5)
+ const mobileAppSeen = request.cookies.get(MOBILE_APP_COOKIE)?.value === "1";
  [FALSE COMMENT #1 REWRITTEN] the old block claiming "The marketing carve-out is for SIGNED-OUT visitors. A signed-in phone at "/" keeps the pre-port behaviour..." is replaced by a "Who owns "/" on a phone" contract: opted out -> desktop UI always wins; presence cookie OR rf-mobile-app -> proxied to the mobile-web build; otherwise (first-time visitor) -> marketing home; every other marketing page, its assets and the auth CTAs are carved out unconditionally.
  const skipMobileRedirect =
    optedOutOfMobile ||
    pathname.startsWith("/api/") ||
    pathname.startsWith("/_next/") ||
+   isMarketingAsset(pathname) ||
+   MARKETING_AUTH_PATH_SET.has(pathname) ||
-   (isMarketingPath(pathname) && !(pathname === "/" && signedIn));
+   (isMarketingPath(pathname) && !(pathname === "/" && (signedIn || mobileAppSeen)));
  (the opAuthed/buyerAuthed reads moved up from the landing block to here; they are the same expressions.)

=== HUNK 5 — middleware.ts, the rewrite itself (A3 marker) ===
- return NextResponse.rewrite(proxyTarget);
+ const proxied = NextResponse.rewrite(proxyTarget);
+ if ((request.headers.get("accept") ?? "").includes("text/html")) {
+   proxied.cookies.set(MOBILE_APP_COOKIE, "1", { httpOnly: true, sameSite: "lax", path: "/", maxAge: MOBILE_APP_COOKIE_MAX_AGE, secure: isProd });
+ }
+ return proxied;
  comment: document requests only - the SPA's own subresource fetches are proxied too and must not mint the marker.

=== HUNK 6 — middleware.ts, the landing-redirect comment (FALSE COMMENT #2 REWRITTEN) ===
- "...the marketing carve-out above applies only to signed-out visitors - so a signed-in phone at "/" was already proxied to the mobile-web build and never reaches this block..."
+ "...Runs AFTER the mobile-UA proxy: a phone carrying either signal the carve-out reads (a presence cookie, or rf-mobile-app) was proxied above and never reaches this block, so a phone that does reach it is either a first-time visitor - signed out, where resolveLandingTarget returns null - or opted out of the mobile build, where landing on /dashboard is what they asked for."

=== HUNK 7 — middleware.ts, dedupe ===
- const isProd = process.env.NODE_ENV === "production";   (the later declaration at the x-tenant-slug block; now hoisted in HUNK 4, same value, same scope)

=== HUNK 8 — app/(marketing)/middleware.marketing.test.ts ===
+ imports MOBILE_APP_COOKIE from ../../middleware and MARKETING_ASSET_FILES / MARKETING_ASSET_PREFIXES / MARKETING_AUTH_PATHS from @/lib/marketing-routes.
+ requestFor() gains an optional `accept` arg; new HTML_ACCEPT const and setCookieFor(response, name) helper reading the real Set-Cookie header.
~ REPLACED the two vacuous signedIn rows: describe "middleware landing carve-out, signed in" -> "returning users", and the two rows that manufactured rf-op-auth / rf-buyer-auth on an iPhone are restated as one it.each over BOTH signals (rf-mobile-app first, then the two presence cookies), with a comment saying plainly that a presence cookie on an iPhone pins a state the Expo build cannot produce and that rf-mobile-app is the signal that population actually carries. "does not proxy / for a signed-out phone" -> "for a first-time phone visitor".
+ (d) rows: / with rf-mobile-app=1 -> rewritten; / with either presence cookie -> rewritten; / bare -> not rewritten.
+ (f) rows: /?desktop=1 with rf-mobile-app=1 -> NOT rewritten; / with prefer-desktop=1 + rf-mobile-app=1 -> NOT rewritten.
+ (a) new describe "marketing assets": 8 paths not rewritten (/brand/*64|180|192|512.png, /marketing/warehouse.webp, /operator-manifest.json, /buyer-manifest.json, /sw.js); 3 counter-pins that the mobile build's OWN /_expo/static/js/web/entry-abc123.js, /manifest.json and /assets/icon.png ARE still rewritten (proves the allow-list is not an extension rule); 1 parity row driving every MARKETING_ASSET_FILES entry and every MARKETING_ASSET_PREFIXES + "probe.png" through the middleware.
+ (b)(c) new describe "auth CTAs": it.each over MARKETING_AUTH_PATHS not rewritten; /login IS rewritten to MOBILE_WEB_URL/login; MARKETING_AUTH_PATHS does not contain /login.
+ (e) new describe "mobile-app marker cookie": proxied document request carries Set-Cookie rf-mobile-app=1 with Path=/, HttpOnly, SameSite=lax, Max-Age=31536000; a proxied non-document request (Accept: */*) carries none; a marketing page served to a phone carries none; a desktop document request carries none.
  The pre-existing MKT-PIN parity row "the middleware exempt set is the pages plus the two metadata routes" is untouched and still green (MARKETING_PATHS deliberately unchanged).
